        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Reading the Label: Sugar, Sweeteners, Protein Source and Extras
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with sugar. Some meal replacements get a large share of their carbohydrate from sugar or fast-digesting starches such as maltodextrin. That is not a disaster in a 400-calorie meal, but a low added-sugar formula with fiber will keep you full longer. Check whether the sweetener is sugar, a non-nutritive sweetener such as sucralose or stevia, or a sugar alcohol such as erythritol, which can cause bloating in some people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, the protein source. Plant-based meal replacements dominate the category, usually built on pea, rice, soy or blends. Soy and well-designed blends provide all essential amino acids, and our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide explains how blends make up for single plant proteins. Plant proteins can carry small amounts of heavy metals from soil, so independent testing is worth looking for; our article on whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> explains what certifications check.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat long superfood lists with skepticism. Adaptogens, mushroom extracts, probiotics and dozens of fruit and vegetable powders sound impressive, but in a single scoop most are present in small amounts that are unlikely to do much. Judge a meal replacement on calories, protein, fiber, fat and vitamins first, and treat the extras as a bonus rather than a reason to pay more. Allergens matter too: check for milk, soy, nuts and gluten if any apply to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="doctor-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Talk to a Doctor First
          </h2>

          <p>
            This guide is not medical advice. Some people should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before replacing meals with shakes. People with diabetes who take insulin or sulfonylureas can have low blood sugar if they cut carbohydrates or calories sharply without adjusting medication. People with kidney disease may need to limit protein, potassium or phosphorus, and many meal replacements are high in all three.
          </p>

          <p>
            People taking warfarin should know that most meal replacements contain added vitamin K, which affects how the drug works; the key is keeping intake consistent, so tell your clinician before adding a daily shake. Pregnant or breastfeeding women need more of certain nutrients and should avoid formulas with herbal extracts, stimulants or high-dose vitamin A; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-pregnancy/">best protein powder for pregnancy</a> guide covers what to look for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Teenagers, older adults with low appetite and anyone with a history of disordered eating should also get personal advice rather than following a shake-based plan alone. Very low calorie programs, including total diet replacement, should only be done under medical supervision. For everyone else, one balanced meal replacement a day alongside normal meals is a reasonable, low-risk way to make a busy schedule easier.
          </p>
        </div>

